* Objects and authorities redb.Route.Tests.IbmMq uses that the IBM MQ developer image (9.4.5.0) does not create.
* The image runs every *.mqsc file in /etc/mqm when the queue manager starts; REPLACE keeps it repeatable.
* The developer configuration already gives user app DEV.** queues and topics (BROWSE GET INQ PUT, PUB SUB),
* DEV.APP.MODEL.QUEUE, and creates DEV.QUEUE.1-3 and DEV.BASE.TOPIC (topic string dev/).

* IbmMqIntegrationTests (Rpc_RequestReply_ReturnsResponse, Producer_ForwardsCustomHeaders, ConcurrentConsumers_*),
* IbmMqXmsListenerTests (Listener_ConcurrentConsumers_*, Listener_Transacted_*): DEV.QUEUE.4.
DEFINE QLOCAL(DEV.QUEUE.4) REPLACE

* IbmMqIntegrationTests (BinaryPayload_RoundTrip, Roundtrip_MultipleMessages), IbmMqTracePropagationTests: DEV.QUEUE.5.
DEFINE QLOCAL(DEV.QUEUE.5) REPLACE

* Request-reply with a temporary reply queue: the MQ-classes producer opens SYSTEM.DEFAULT.MODEL.QUEUE, and so does
* XMS for a temporary queue (IbmMqIntegrationTests.Rpc_*, IbmMqHeaderFilterStrategyTests.OnARequestReplyProducer_*,
* OnAReplyingConsumer_*, IbmMqXmsListenerTests.Listener_Rpc*). The creator of the dynamic queue owns it.
SET AUTHREC PROFILE(SYSTEM.DEFAULT.MODEL.QUEUE) OBJTYPE(QUEUE) PRINCIPAL('app') AUTHADD(DSP,GET,PUT,INQ)

* IbmMqIntegrationTests.Topic_PublishSubscribe publishes to DEV/TEST/EVENTS; DEV.BASE.TOPIC covers dev/ only.
* A topic object for the node DEV (no trailing slash: 'DEV/' would be an empty level under it) falls under the
* DEV.** topic authority of user app.
DEFINE TOPIC(DEV.TEST.TOPIC) TOPICSTR('DEV') REPLACE
